{
  "commit": "77b4c03d462c70d0d805445206fc0daa7b2f262c",
  "contamination_note": "A first attempt was discarded. A Gazebo stack from an earlier killed run survived about seven hours and a second stack was launched alongside it, so two simulators published /clock during the 0.20 measurement. That record is quarantined as CONTAMINATED_two_simulators_rate_0.20.json and is not used. The sweep harness now aborts unless the process count is zero before each launch.",
  "control_check": "The 0.35 run reproduces studies/gate0/gate0_item1_audit.json (+0.8522/-0.8307 rad over 3.948 s) within run-to-run variation, so the --yaw-rate parameter did not disturb the gate path.",
  "design_kind": "engineering_rotation_authority_sweep",
  "eliminated_causes": [
    "commanded rate: 0.20-0.35 rad/s all rotate from rest in both worlds, and 0.166 rad/s worked inside the stalled run itself",
    "world, ground friction and geometry: the retained smoke world rotates as well as the qualification world",
    "attitude, ride height and suspension: core z 0.1780, roll and pitch 0.0000, pod suspension 0.0020 m, identical while turning and while stalled",
    "actuator stall: wheels measured 0.91 of 0.99 rad/s commanded during the stall",
    "wheels off the ground: suspension travel and pod z identical between the two states"
  ],
  "evidence_class": "engineering_debug_never_pilot_or_confirmatory",
  "finding": "No commanded-rate floor exists. Every rate from 0.20 to 0.35 rad/s rotated the assembly from rest, at 58-75% of command, with no knee. This refutes the (0.271, 0.350] breakaway bracket recorded on 2026-09-25, which had inferred a threshold from one stalled run plus the Gate 0 figure. In particular 0.27 rad/s rotates here at 64% of command, while 0.271 rad/s produced 0.0000 rad over 29.5 s twice in the station-smoke run. The station-smoke stall is therefore specific to that run rather than a property of the commanded rate.",
  "heading_dependence": {
    "commanded_yaw_rate_radps": 0.271,
    "consequences": "If the hypothesis holds, every signed-motion, travel and reconfiguration figure the platform has recorded is heading-dependent, and Gate 0 item 1 is collected at the spawn heading near zero, which is the most favourable one. Confirmatory layouts route the robot in all directions. This is the same class of defect as the wall-clock command window.",
    "finding": "Rotation authority depends on the heading of the robot in the world, with a period of 180 degrees. It is 65 percent of command at yaw -0.03, 58-59 percent at -0.8, EXACTLY ZERO at both +1.571 and -1.571, and back to 65 and 64 percent at +3.142. The two 90-degree runs return identical straight-stage translation of 0.5830 m, consistent with a deterministic axis swap. Position is not the variable: the original stall pose rotates at 65 and 64 percent when only its yaw is changed to zero. Teleporting is not the variable: a null teleport gives 65 percent and a teleport to yaw -0.8 gives 58 percent.",
    "not_yet_proven": "This is a hypothesis confirmed by signature, not by intervention. The decisive test is to set fdir1 on the wheel collisions, or make the wheel friction isotropic, and re-run the plus and minus 90 degree cases. That is a platform change touching every recorded motion figure, so it is an author decision and is deliberately not made here.",
    "protocol": "Impose a pose, settle 2 s, then run the declared stage sequence. A null teleport to the unchanged spawn pose and a teleport to yaw -0.8 are the controls that rule out the teleport itself as the cause.",
    "root_cause_hypothesis": "The drive pod wheels declare anisotropic Coulomb friction, mu 1.2 and mu2 0.35, in src/modular_robot_sim/models/drive_pod/model.sdf lines 10-11, and fdir1 is never specified anywhere in the repository. Without fdir1 the friction principal axes are not attached to the wheel, so which coefficient resists rolling and which resists lateral scrub depends on the orientation of the robot in the world. That predicts exactly the 180-degree periodicity and the collapse at plus and minus 90 degrees that is measured here. The allocation itself is innocent: modular_robot_bringup/kinematics.py distribute_twist works purely in body frame and cannot depend on world heading, and the pod commands recorded during the original stall match the body-frame prediction.",
